                        "httpStatus":  200,
                        "status":  "NOT_PROVEN",
                        "response":  "- On receipt: make a defensive copy of any untrusted input, then normalize the copied string before validating it. Normalization is important because in Unicode the same string can have many different representations; normalizing first ensures your validation sees a consistent form.\n\n- Validate inputs before use (after any defensive copying and normalization). Validation should cover the kinds of problems named in the policy (for example, integer overflows and directory traversal attempts using \"../\"). Blacklisting (for example forbidding \"\u003cscript\u003e\" tags) can be a useful part of a strategy but is not sufficient by itself.\n\n- Before writing user-controlled text to logs, sanitize and validate it. Unsanitized user input in logs can enable log injection (for example an attacker inserting a CRLF to split a log entry) and can leak sensitive data across trust boundaries (for example an attacker injecting a script into a log file that, when viewed in a browser, could expose an administrator’s cookie).",
