                        "httpStatus":  200,
                        "status":  "NOT_PROVEN",
                        "response":  "Making classes and methods final\n- Prevents dangerous subclassing: a non‑final class or method lets an attacker extend it in “a dangerous and unforeseen way.”  \n- Blocks specific subclass attacks: making a class final “prevents a malicious subclass from adding finalizers, cloning, and overriding random methods.”\n\nApplying least privilege\n- Reduces impact of coding flaws: code “operating with reduced privileges” makes exploitation of flaws “likely to be thwarted.”  \n- Enforces limits at runtime: restricting permissions via policy files and using java.security.AccessController.doPrivileged, or running RIAs sandboxed (so attempts to execute security‑sensitive code cause a security exception), keeps components from performing sensitive actions they don’t need.  \n- Advises minimizing granted permissions: RIAs “should follow the principle of least privilege” and be configured with the least necessary permissions; “running a RIA with all permissions should be avoided whenever possible.”\n\nExplicitly acknowledged extensibility trade‑off\n- “This causes a loss of extensibility, in exchange for security.”",
