                        "httpStatus":  200,
                        "status":  "NOT_PROVEN",
                        "response":  "- Minimize programmer-caused vulnerabilities by adhering to recommended secure coding guidelines; EPAM’s Java guidance combines Oracle’s official guidelines and community rules and recommendations to achieve this goal.  \n- Document security-related information in API documentation (for example, JavaDoc), including required permissions, security-related exceptions, caller sensitivity, and any security-relevant preconditions or postconditions.  \n- Design and write code that is obviously safe rather than relying on clever logic to prove safety; follow the guidelines in this document unless there is a very strong reason not to.  \n- Design APIs with security in mind (rather than retrofitting security); for example, make classes final to prevent malicious subclasses from adding finalizers, cloning, or overriding methods, and treat any use of the SecurityManager as an area that should be scrutinized.  \n- Minimize the number of permission checks: treat SecurityManager checks as a last resort, perform security checks at a few defined points, and return an object (a capability) that client code retains so no further permission checks are required.",
